Keep the two classes this kernelcache lacks out of matching

run-377 and run-378 both end on M33/2 IOHIDResource after M32/2
AppleMobileFileIntegrity, with the candidate dropped (mx23) and no probe call:
neither class exists in this kernel image, so their candidate lists come back
r=5,5 every round and the same nubs return to the queue for ever.

Skip matching for both by name, and say SKIP <name> on the framebuffer so the
skip is visible.  Reversible: authentication is already off on the command line
and the touch path is not connected yet.
---
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index ddfa5c2..7870f25 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -1119,10 +1119,29 @@ IOService::startMatching( IOOptionBits options )
 	 * The CPU interrupt controller has no driver to match against and only
 	 * needs to be linked and announced, so skip matching for it once, and
 	 * say so. */
-	if (getProvider() == NULL && getName()
-	    && !strcmp(getName(), "IOCPUInterruptController")) {
-		printf("\nnio: SM skip cpuic");
-		return;
+	/* nio: the three names that cannot settle.  The CPU interrupt
+	 * controller re-enters matching from a kext start; AppleMobileFileIntegrity
+	 * and IOHIDResource are Apple classes this kernelcache does not carry, and
+	 * their candidate lists end r=5,5 every round, so the same nubs come back
+	 * and the loop never drains.  Skipping is reversible: authentication is
+	 * already off on the command line and the touch path is not connected. */
+	if (getName() != NULL) {
+		const char * nio_skip_names[] = {
+			"AppleMobileFileIntegrity",
+			"IOHIDResource",
+		};
+		for (unsigned nio_i = 0; nio_i < 2; nio_i++) {
+			if (!strcmp(getName(), nio_skip_names[nio_i])) {
+				nio_u_mark("\nSKIP ");
+				nio_u_mark(nio_skip_names[nio_i]);
+				return;
+			}
+		}
+		if (getProvider() == NULL
+		    && !strcmp(getName(), "IOCPUInterruptController")) {
+			printf("\nnio: SM skip cpuic");
+			return;
+		}
 	}
 
 	/* nio: how many matching rounds run, against how many services start. */
